Package: must
Title: Multiple-Stressor Interactions Under Alternative Null Models
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Pablo", "Amador Crespo", email = "pamadorc@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-3793-8794")),
    person("Andreu", "Rico", role = "aut",
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-1820-4218"))
  )
Description: Detects and classifies non-additive effects of multiple stressors in
    experiments, testing each combination of stressors against alternative
    null models with the same criteria. Before any model is fitted, the design
    is described: missing treatment combinations, cells shared by several
    contrasts, replication units and repeated measures, and which
    interactions it identifies. Interactions are tested from the adjusted
    predictions of one model per response, following the post-estimation
    inference framework of Madge Pimentel et al. (2025)
    <doi:10.1002/ece3.71959>, by default with a small-sample correction
    (restricted maximum likelihood and t distributions with the degrees of
    freedom of the experimental units). The model is either a generalised
    linear mixed model fitted to each response, with its error distribution
    chosen by convergence, simulation-based residual checks and AIC, or one
    fitted with another package and supported by 'emmeans'. The null models
    are simple addition (the default, which flagged the fewest false
    interactions across mechanisms of no interaction in simulations), the
    multiplicative and dominance models, Bliss independence, with the
    emergent three-way interaction of Beppler et al. (2016)
    <doi:10.1098/rsif.2016.0800>, the stress addition model of Liess et al.
    (2016) <doi:10.1038/srep32965> and concentration addition. Combinations
    with only zeros are tested from the probability of that outcome. For
    three or more stressors the net deviation from the single-stressor
    effects is distinguished from the pure highest-order interaction.
    Interactions can be classified with the directional scheme of Piggott et
    al. (2015) <doi:10.1002/ece3.1465>.
